WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.512
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 2,
and the slide shows part of its

2
00:00:03.512 --> 00:00:07.575
condensed decision table, which
is the program's design guidance.

3
00:00:07.575 --> 00:00:12.487
Anything disabled, unused or with
no owner after Step 1 is retired.

4
00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:15.480
A traditional account for
an Azure workload goes

5
00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.326
to a managed identity,
user-assigned preferred.

6
00:00:18.326 --> 00:00:22.080
One calling Entra-protected A
P I's from a host with an O I D

7
00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:26.325
C issuer gets an app registration
with a federated credential.

8
00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:31.496
A Kerberos-only account on premises goes
to a group managed service account,

9
00:00:31.496 --> 00:00:34.862
or a delegated one on a
Windows Server 2025 domain.

10
00:00:34.862 --> 00:00:39.387
A managed service account is upgraded
to a group managed one first, because,

11
00:00:39.387 --> 00:00:44.058
as Microsoft Learn's delegated managed
service account documentation says,

12
00:00:44.058 --> 00:00:46.612
neither can be migrated
to a delegated one.

13
00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:50.120
And a client secret is
replaced: a managed identity,

14
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.625
then federation, then a certificate.

15
00:00:52.625 --> 00:00:54.375
Two more points from Step 2.

16
00:00:54.375 --> 00:00:56.137
High-risk accounts go first.

17
00:00:56.137 --> 00:01:01.168
And a group managed account needs a K
D S root key, created once per domain;

18
00:01:01.168 --> 00:01:05.004
Microsoft Learn's Create a
K D S root key documentation

19
00:01:05.004 --> 00:01:08.980
says domain controllers wait
up to ten hours after that.
